// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 4): technology and space.
ADDART({id:"telegraph",t:"The Telegraph and Morse Code",c:"Technology",i:"📠",w:"Electrical telegraph",r:["phone","radio","elec","subseacables","indrev","morsecode"],
p:[["Messages by wire","The electric telegraph sent messages along wires using electrical pulses."],["Dots and dashes","Morse code turned letters into short and long signals."],["The Victorian internet","Telegraph cables linked the world in the 19th century."]],
l:"The electric telegraph was the first technology to send messages almost instantly over long distances, using electrical signals along wires. Before it, news traveled only as fast as a horse, ship or train. Several inventors developed telegraphs in the 1830s, including William Cooke and Charles Wheatstone in Britain, whose system went into use on a railway in 1838, and Samuel Morse in the United States. Morse and Alfred Vail created Morse code, which represents letters and numbers as combinations of short and long signals, dots and dashes. On 24 May 1844, Morse sent the message ‘What hath God wrought’ from Washington, D.C., to Baltimore. Telegraph lines spread rapidly along railways, and a lasting transatlantic cable was laid in 1866. Telegrams carried news, business deals, diplomatic messages and personal greetings, and telegraph networks have been called the ‘Victorian internet’. The telephone, radio and later the internet replaced the telegraph; Western Union sent its last telegram in 2006. Morse code is still used by some radio amateurs.",
s:[["How it worked","Pressing a telegraph key closed an electric circuit, sending current down the wire. At the other end, an electromagnet moved a lever that clicked or marked paper tape. Skilled operators could send and read about 30 to 40 words a minute by ear."],["Morse code","Morse code gives the most common letters the shortest codes: E is a single dot and T a single dash. International Morse code, adopted in 1865, became standard worldwide. The distress signal SOS, three dots, three dashes, three dots, was adopted in 1906 because it was easy to send and recognize."],["Changing the world","The telegraph let newspapers report events from distant places on the same day, helped railways run safely, allowed businesses to trade across continents and changed warfare and diplomacy. Time zones and standardized time were partly driven by telegraph timing signals."],["Wireless telegraphy","In the 1890s, Guglielmo Marconi developed wireless telegraphy using radio waves, and in 1901 he received signals across the Atlantic. Ships carried wireless operators, and the Titanic’s distress calls in 1912 brought rescuers. Ships stopped officially using Morse code for distress in 1999."]],
k:[["Key inventors","Cooke and Wheatstone; Morse and Vail"],["First Morse message","24 May 1844"],["Code","Morse code (dots and dashes)"],["Lasting transatlantic cable","1866"],["Last Western Union telegram","2006"]],
d:[["1837","Cooke and Wheatstone patent an electric telegraph"],["1844","Morse sends ‘What hath God wrought’"],["1858","First transatlantic telegraph cable works briefly"],["1866","Lasting transatlantic cable laid"],["1906","SOS adopted as a distress signal"],["2006","Western Union ends its telegram service"]],
s2:[["Telegram style","Because telegrams were charged by the word, people wrote short, clipped messages, leaving out small words and using STOP instead of a period. This ‘telegraphese’ influenced newspaper headlines and later text messaging."],["The Pony Express","The Pony Express carried mail by horse relay across the western United States for only 18 months before the transcontinental telegraph was completed in October 1861, making it obsolete."]],
sp:["What hath God wrought","The first official Morse message, a Bible verse chosen by Annie Ellsworth, the daughter of a patent official, was sent from the US Capitol to Alfred Vail in Baltimore on 24 May 1844."],
g:[["Telegraph","A system for sending messages by electric signals"],["Morse code","A code of short and long signals"],["Telegram","A message sent by telegraph"],["Telegraph key","A switch used to send Morse code"]],
f:["Morse code’s shortest letter is E, a single dot.","SOS became the distress signal in 1906.","Western Union sent its last telegram in 2006."],
fun:"Skilled Morse code operators can recognize each other’s ‘fist’, the personal rhythm of their sending, like a handwriting style."});

ADDART({id:"email",t:"Email",c:"Technology",i:"📧",w:"Email",r:["internet","comp","passwords","cybersecurity","socialmedia"],
p:[["Electronic letters","Email sends messages between computers over networks."],["The @ sign","Ray Tomlinson chose @ for email addresses in 1971."],["Billions of users","More than 4 billion people use email, and spam is a constant problem."]],
l:"Email, short for electronic mail, is a method of sending messages from one person to another over computer networks. Early forms of electronic messaging existed on shared computers in the 1960s, but in 1971 American engineer Ray Tomlinson sent the first email between two computers on ARPANET, the forerunner of the internet, and chose the @ symbol to separate the user’s name from the computer’s name. Email became the most popular use of ARPANET, spread through universities and businesses in the 1980s and reached the public in the 1990s with services such as Hotmail (1996) and later Gmail (2004). Today more than 4 billion people use email, sending hundreds of billions of messages a day. Email remains essential for work, online accounts and official communication, even as messaging apps have replaced it for many personal conversations. Unwanted bulk messages, called spam, make up a large share of email traffic, and phishing emails are a major way criminals steal passwords and spread malware.",
s:[["How email works","An email is sent from an email program or website to a mail server, which looks up the recipient’s domain, the part after the @, and passes the message to the recipient’s server using a standard called SMTP. The recipient then downloads or views it using standards such as IMAP or POP."],["Spam","The first spam email was sent in 1978 by Gary Thuerk, a marketing manager who advertised computers to about 400 ARPANET users, causing complaints. The word ‘spam’ comes from a Monty Python comedy sketch in which the canned meat Spam is endlessly repeated. Filters now block most spam automatically."],["Phishing","Phishing emails pretend to come from banks, companies or colleagues to trick people into clicking links, opening attachments or revealing passwords. Signs include urgent requests, unusual sender addresses and links that do not match the claimed website."],["Email etiquette","Email developed conventions such as subject lines, signatures and the ‘reply all’ button, which has caused embarrassing mistakes. Writing in capital letters is considered shouting. Many workplaces now try to limit after-hours email."]],
k:[["First network email","Ray Tomlinson, 1971"],["Symbol","@ separates user and server"],["First spam","1978"],["Users","More than 4 billion"],["Main sending standard","SMTP"]],
d:[["1965","Messages sent between users of one computer at MIT"],["1971","Ray Tomlinson sends the first network email"],["1978","First spam email"],["1982","SMTP email standard published"],["1996","Hotmail launches free web email"],["2004","Gmail launches"]],
s2:[["The @ sign","Before email, the @ symbol was used mainly in accounting to mean ‘at the rate of’. Tomlinson chose it because it was on the keyboard and unlikely to appear in names. It now has many nicknames, such as ‘snail’ in Italian and ‘little monkey’ in several languages."],["Email’s carbon footprint","Sending and storing emails uses electricity in data centers. A single email has a tiny footprint, but the vast number sent, especially with large attachments, adds up, which has led to campaigns to delete old messages and unsubscribe from newsletters."]],
sp:["Queen Elizabeth’s email","In 1976, Queen Elizabeth II sent an email from a British army research center using ARPANET, making her one of the first heads of state to send one."],
g:[["Email address","A name and domain separated by @"],["Spam","Unwanted bulk email"],["Phishing","Tricking people with fake messages"],["Server","A computer that stores and delivers data"]],
f:["Ray Tomlinson chose @ for email addresses.","The first spam was sent in 1978.","More than 4 billion people use email."],
fun:"Ray Tomlinson said he could not remember the text of the first email, which was probably a test such as ‘QWERTYUIOP’."});

ADDART({id:"fasteners",t:"Zippers and Velcro",c:"Technology",i:"🤐",w:"Zipper",r:["clothing","sewingmachine","plastics","spaceagencies","astronauts"],
p:[["Closing clothes","Zippers and hook-and-loop fasteners close clothes, bags and shoes."],["A slow start","The zipper took decades to become popular."],["Inspired by burrs","Velcro was invented after a Swiss engineer studied burrs stuck to his dog."]],
l:"Zippers and hook-and-loop fasteners are two of the most widely used ways of fastening clothing, bags, shoes and other items. A zipper has two strips of interlocking teeth that are joined or separated by a slider. Early versions were patented by Elias Howe in 1851 and Whitcomb Judson, whose ‘clasp locker’ appeared at the 1893 Chicago World’s Fair, but they often jammed. Swedish-American engineer Gideon Sundback designed the modern zipper in 1913, and the name ‘zipper’ came from a brand of rubber boots in 1923. Zippers became common on trousers and jackets in the 1930s. Hook-and-loop fasteners were invented by Swiss engineer George de Mestral, who noticed burdock burrs sticking to his dog’s fur after a walk in 1941 and saw under a microscope that they had tiny hooks. He developed a fabric with hooks on one side and loops on the other, patented in 1955 and sold under the brand Velcro. It is a famous example of biomimicry, inventions inspired by nature, and was used by NASA astronauts.",
s:[["How a zipper works","The slider has a Y-shaped channel. Moving it up forces the teeth on each side together at an angle so that each tooth’s hook fits into the hollow of a tooth opposite. Moving it down wedges them apart. Zippers can be made of metal, molded plastic or plastic coils."],["Hook and loop","One strip has thousands of tiny stiff hooks, and the other has soft loops. Pressed together, the hooks catch the loops; pulling them apart makes the familiar ripping sound. Modern versions can be opened and closed thousands of times."],["Zippers take over","The US military used zippers on flight suits and boots in World War I. In the 1930s, fashion designers such as Elsa Schiaparelli used them prominently, and they replaced buttons on men’s trousers. Japan’s YKK became the world’s largest zipper maker."],["Velcro in space","NASA used hook-and-loop fasteners in the Apollo program to keep objects from floating away in weightlessness, attaching pens, food packets and equipment to walls. Astronauts even used a patch inside their helmets to scratch their noses."]],
k:[["Modern zipper","Gideon Sundback, 1913"],["Name ‘zipper’","1923"],["Hook-and-loop inventor","George de Mestral"],["Inspiration","Burdock burrs"],["Velcro patent","1955"]],
d:[["1851","Elias Howe patents an ‘automatic continuous clothing closure’"],["1893","Judson’s clasp locker shown in Chicago"],["1913","Sundback designs the modern zipper"],["1923","The name ‘zipper’ is used for boots"],["1941","De Mestral studies burrs on his dog"],["1955","Hook-and-loop fastener patented"]],
s2:[["The name Velcro","The name Velcro combines the French words velours, ‘velvet’, and crochet, ‘hook’. The company has asked people to say ‘hook and loop’ rather than using its brand name for all such fasteners, even releasing a humorous song about it in 2017."],["Zipper mishaps","Doctors treat a number of zipper injuries each year, mostly from trousers. Engineers have designed zippers with safety features, and YKK makes zippers resistant to water for space suits and diving gear."]],
sp:["Biomimicry","Biomimicry means copying designs from nature. Besides Velcro, examples include swimsuits inspired by sharkskin, self-cleaning surfaces inspired by lotus leaves and train noses inspired by kingfisher beaks."],
g:[["Fastener","A device that joins two things"],["Slider","The part that opens and closes a zipper"],["Burr","A seed head with hooks that sticks to fur"],["Biomimicry","Designs inspired by nature"]],
f:["The modern zipper was designed in 1913.","Velcro was inspired by burrs on a dog.","Astronauts used Velcro to scratch their noses."],
fun:"George de Mestral’s idea took about ten years to perfect, because making tiny, strong hooks from fabric was very difficult until he used nylon."});

ADDART({id:"subseacables",t:"Undersea Internet Cables",c:"Technology",i:"🌊",w:"Submarine communications cable",r:["internet","fiberoptics","telegraph","trench","cybersecurity"],
p:[["The internet’s backbone","Most data between continents travels through cables on the seabed."],["Hundreds of cables","About 600 cable systems are in service around the world."],["Vulnerable","Ship anchors, earthquakes and sabotage can cut cables."]],
l:"Undersea, or submarine, communications cables are cables laid on the seabed to carry telecommunications signals between continents and islands. Although many people imagine the internet travels by satellite, about 99 percent of international data, including web pages, video calls and financial transactions, flows through these cables. Modern cables contain optical fibers that carry data as pulses of laser light, with repeaters every 50 to 100 kilometers to boost the signal, and are only about as thick as a garden hose in deep water. As of 2026, the research firm TeleGeography counted about 600 cable systems in service and about 100 planned, stretching well over a million kilometers. Undersea cables began with telegraph cables: the first crossed the English Channel in 1850, and a lasting transatlantic cable was laid in 1866. Today large technology companies such as Google, Meta, Amazon and Microsoft own or fund many new cables. Cables are damaged about 150 to 200 times a year, mostly by fishing gear and ship anchors, and suspected sabotage has raised security concerns.",
s:[["Laying cables","Special cable ships carry thousands of kilometers of cable and lay it as they sail, following routes carefully surveyed to avoid steep slopes and hazards. Near shore, cables are armored and buried in the seabed to protect them from anchors and trawlers. In deep water, they rest on the ocean floor."],["How fast?","A single modern cable can carry hundreds of terabits of data per second, enough to stream millions of high-definition videos at once. The first fiber-optic transatlantic cable, TAT-8, opened in 1988."],["Breaks and repairs","When a cable breaks, traffic is rerouted through other cables, and repair ships use grapnels to lift the cable from the seabed and splice it. A 2006 earthquake off Taiwan broke several cables and disrupted internet service across East Asia. In 2024, cables in the Red Sea were cut after a ship damaged by an attack dragged its anchor."],["Security","Because cables are essential to economies, governments worry about sabotage and spying. Several incidents of cut cables in the Baltic Sea in 2023–2025 led NATO to increase patrols. Countries are building more routes to make networks resilient."]],
k:[["Share of international data","About 99%"],["Cable systems in service","About 600 (2026)"],["Technology","Optical fibers"],["First undersea cable","English Channel, 1850"],["First fiber-optic transatlantic cable","TAT-8, 1988"]],
d:[["1850","First undersea telegraph cable crosses the English Channel"],["1866","Lasting transatlantic telegraph cable"],["1956","First transatlantic telephone cable, TAT-1"],["1988","First fiber-optic transatlantic cable, TAT-8"],["2006","Earthquake off Taiwan cuts several cables"],["2024","Red Sea cable cuts disrupt traffic"]],
s2:[["Sharks and cables","In the 1980s, sharks were found to have bitten some fiber-optic cables, possibly attracted by electrical fields. Cable makers added protective layers, and shark bites are now a very minor cause of damage."],["The Great Eastern","The huge steamship Great Eastern, the only ship big enough to carry an entire transatlantic cable, laid the successful 1866 cable and recovered and completed a lost 1865 cable."]],
sp:["Tonga cut off","In January 2022, the eruption of the Hunga Tonga–Hunga Haʻapai volcano severed Tonga’s only international cable, cutting the country off from the internet for about five weeks until it was repaired."],
g:[["Optical fiber","A thin glass strand that carries light"],["Repeater","A device that boosts a signal along a cable"],["Grapnel","A hook used to lift cables from the seabed"],["Bandwidth","The amount of data that can be carried"]],
f:["About 99% of international data travels through undersea cables.","About 600 cable systems are in service.","Most cable damage is caused by fishing and anchors."],
fun:"In deep water, an internet cable crossing an ocean may be only about as thick as a garden hose."});

ADDART({id:"searchengines",t:"Search Engines",c:"Technology",i:"🔍",w:"Search engine",r:["internet","ai","dataprivacy","recommender","misinformation"],
p:[["Finding information","Search engines help people find pages on the web."],["Crawl, index, rank","They copy the web, organize it and rank the results."],["One giant","Google handles about 90 percent of searches worldwide."]],
l:"A search engine is a software system that helps people find information on the World Wide Web. When a user types a query, the search engine returns a ranked list of web pages, images, videos, news and other results, usually in a fraction of a second. Search engines work in three stages: automated programs called crawlers or spiders visit and copy billions of web pages; the pages are stored and organized in a giant index; and ranking algorithms decide which results are most relevant to each query, considering hundreds of factors such as words on the page, links from other sites and the user’s location. Early search tools included Archie (1990), Yahoo’s directory (1994) and AltaVista (1995). Google, founded in 1998, became dominant with its PageRank system, which ranked pages by the links pointing to them, and now handles about 90 percent of searches worldwide. Other search engines include Microsoft’s Bing, Baidu in China, Yandex in Russia and Naver in South Korea. Since 2023, search engines have increasingly added AI-generated answers.",
s:[["PageRank","Larry Page and Sergey Brin, PhD students at Stanford University, developed PageRank, which treated a link from one page to another as a vote, giving more weight to votes from important pages. Combined with a simple, fast interface, it made Google’s results much better than rivals’."],["Advertising","Most search engines are free to use and earn money by showing advertisements linked to search terms. Google’s parent company, Alphabet, earns most of its revenue from advertising. Businesses use search engine optimization (SEO) to try to rank higher in results."],["Antitrust","In August 2024, a US federal judge ruled that Google had illegally maintained a monopoly in search, partly by paying billions of dollars to be the default search engine on phones and browsers. In 2025 the judge ordered remedies including sharing some data with competitors but did not force Google to sell its Chrome browser; both sides appealed. The European Union has also fined Google."],["AI and search","Since 2023, search engines have added AI chatbots and summaries that answer questions directly. Supporters say this saves time; critics worry about errors, reduced traffic to websites that create content, and the concentration of information in a few companies."]],
k:[["Stages","Crawl, index, rank"],["Google founded","1998"],["Google’s share","About 90% of global searches"],["Other major engines","Bing, Baidu, Yandex, Naver"],["Key ranking idea","PageRank (links as votes)"]],
d:[["1990","Archie, the first internet search tool"],["1994","Yahoo’s web directory launches"],["1995","AltaVista launches"],["1998","Google founded"],["2009","Microsoft launches Bing"],["2024","US court rules Google illegally maintained a search monopoly"]],
s2:[["To google","The verb ‘to google’ was added to the Oxford English Dictionary and Merriam-Webster in 2006. Google has asked people to use it only for searches made with Google."],["Filter bubbles","Personalized search results based on location and history can show different people different results. Some researchers worry this could create ‘filter bubbles’ that reinforce existing beliefs, though studies suggest the effect in search is smaller than often claimed."]],
sp:["The name Google","Google’s name is a play on ‘googol’, the number 1 followed by 100 zeros, reflecting the founders’ goal of organizing a huge amount of information."],
g:[["Crawler","A program that visits and copies web pages"],["Index","An organized database of web pages"],["Algorithm","A set of rules a computer follows"],["SEO","Search engine optimization"]],
f:["Google handles about 90 percent of searches worldwide.","Search engines work by crawling, indexing and ranking.","Google’s name comes from ‘googol’."],
fun:"Google receives billions of searches every day, and about 15 percent of searches each day have never been searched before."});

ADDART({id:"datacenters",t:"Data Centers",c:"Technology",i:"🏢",w:"Data center",r:["cloud","ai","internet","elec","waterscarcity"],
p:[["Where the internet lives","Data centers are buildings full of computers that store and process data."],["Power-hungry","They use about 1.5 percent of the world’s electricity, and demand is rising fast."],["Driven by AI","Artificial intelligence is fueling a boom in data center building."]],
l:"A data center is a building or group of buildings housing computer servers, data storage and networking equipment that store, process and deliver digital information. Every web search, video stream, email, online purchase and AI chatbot response relies on data centers. Large ‘hyperscale’ data centers run by companies such as Amazon, Microsoft, Google and Meta can contain hundreds of thousands of servers and cover areas the size of several football fields. Data centers need huge amounts of electricity to run and cool their equipment: the International Energy Agency estimated that they used about 415 terawatt-hours in 2024, about 1.5 percent of global electricity, that demand grew 17 percent in 2025, and that it could roughly double by 2030, driven largely by artificial intelligence. Many also use large amounts of water for cooling. The United States has the most data centers, followed by China and Europe. Their growth has brought investment and jobs but also concerns about electricity prices, grid strain, emissions, water use and local land.",
s:[["Inside a data center","Servers are stacked in tall racks arranged in rows, connected by fiber-optic cables. Backup batteries and generators keep them running during power cuts. Security guards, cameras and access controls protect the equipment, and the most important data is copied to several sites."],["Keeping cool","Computers turn electricity into heat. Data centers use fans, air conditioning, chilled water and increasingly liquid cooling, in which coolant flows directly over hot chips. Some are built in cold places such as Scandinavia, and a few reuse waste heat to warm nearby homes."],["AI and power","Training and running large AI models uses powerful chips called GPUs, which consume much more electricity than ordinary servers. Technology companies have signed deals for solar, wind, geothermal and nuclear power, including plans to restart closed nuclear plants, while some new data centers rely on natural gas."],["History","Early computers of the 1940s and 1950s filled whole rooms. Businesses later kept servers in their own ‘server rooms’. In the 2000s, cloud computing, led by Amazon Web Services from 2006, moved computing into giant shared data centers."]],
k:[["Electricity use (2024)","About 415 TWh, about 1.5% of the world total"],["Growth in 2025","17% (IEA)"],["Outlook","Roughly double by 2030"],["Largest market","United States"],["Main driver","Artificial intelligence"]],
d:[["1946","ENIAC fills a large room"],["1990s","Internet boom drives early data centers"],["2006","Amazon Web Services launches cloud computing"],["2018","Microsoft tests an underwater data center"],["2025","Data center electricity use rises 17%"]],
s2:[["Underwater data center","In 2018, Microsoft sank a sealed data center the size of a shipping container off Scotland’s Orkney Islands. After two years, its servers had failed less often than those on land, though the company later said it would not build more."],["Local debates","In places such as northern Virginia, the world’s largest data center hub, Ireland and the Netherlands, rapid data center growth has led to debates about electricity supplies and land use. Ireland’s data centers used about a fifth of the country’s electricity by the mid-2020s."]],
sp:["Waste heat","In Stockholm, Helsinki and other northern cities, heat from data centers is piped into district heating networks, warming thousands of homes."],
g:[["Server","A computer that provides data or services to others"],["Hyperscale","Extremely large-scale computing facilities"],["Terawatt-hour","A billion kilowatt-hours of electricity"],["GPU","A powerful processor used for graphics and AI"]],
f:["Data centers use about 1.5% of the world’s electricity.","AI is driving rapid growth in data center power use.","Some data centers heat nearby homes."],
fun:"Northern Virginia, near Washington, D.C., has so many data centers that a large share of the world’s internet traffic is thought to pass through the area."});

ADDART({id:"thermometers",t:"Thermometers",c:"Technology",i:"🌡️",w:"Thermometer",r:["thermo","fever","climate","galileo","mercury"],
p:[["Measuring temperature","Thermometers measure how hot or cold something is."],["Three famous scales","Fahrenheit, Celsius and Kelvin are the main temperature scales."],["Leaving mercury behind","Mercury thermometers are being phased out because mercury is toxic."]],
l:"A thermometer is an instrument for measuring temperature. Around 1593, Galileo Galilei built a thermoscope, a glass bulb and tube in which water rose and fell as air expanded and contracted, but it had no scale. Sealed liquid-in-glass thermometers were developed in Italy in the 1650s. German-Dutch instrument maker Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit made the first reliable mercury thermometer in 1714 and created his temperature scale in 1724. Swedish astronomer Anders Celsius proposed a scale based on water’s freezing and boiling points in 1742, and Lord Kelvin introduced an absolute scale starting at absolute zero in 1848. Thermometers are used in medicine to check for fever, in weather forecasting, cooking, industry and science. Modern types include digital thermometers, infrared thermometers that measure heat radiation without contact, and thermocouples. Because mercury is toxic, many countries have phased out mercury thermometers under the Minamata Convention, which entered into force in 2017.",
s:[["How they work","Liquid thermometers rely on the fact that liquids expand when heated. Bimetallic strips, made of two metals that expand at different rates, bend as temperature changes. Digital thermometers use sensors whose electrical resistance changes with temperature. Infrared thermometers detect the heat energy that all objects radiate."],["Temperature scales","On the Celsius scale, water freezes at 0° and boils at 100° at sea level. On the Fahrenheit scale, used mainly in the United States, water freezes at 32° and boils at 212°. The Kelvin scale, used in science, begins at absolute zero, −273.15°C, the coldest possible temperature. The scales meet at −40°, where Celsius and Fahrenheit are equal."],["Medical thermometers","In 1867, English doctor Thomas Clifford Allbutt invented a short clinical thermometer that took a temperature in five minutes instead of 20. Normal body temperature is around 37°C, though it varies between people and through the day. Ear and forehead thermometers became common in the 2000s and during the COVID-19 pandemic."],["Weather records","Standard weather thermometers are kept in shaded, ventilated boxes about 1.5 meters above the ground. Long thermometer records, such as the Central England Temperature series starting in 1659, help scientists track climate change."]],
k:[["Early thermoscope","Galileo, about 1593"],["Mercury thermometer","Fahrenheit, 1714"],["Celsius scale","1742"],["Kelvin scale","1848"],["Mercury phase-out","Minamata Convention (in force 2017)"]],
d:[["c. 1593","Galileo’s thermoscope"],["1654","Sealed liquid thermometer made in Florence"],["1714","Fahrenheit’s mercury thermometer"],["1742","Celsius proposes his scale"],["1848","Kelvin proposes an absolute scale"],["1867","Allbutt’s clinical thermometer"],["2017","Minamata Convention on mercury enters into force"]],
s2:[["Celsius upside down","Anders Celsius’s original scale was reversed: water boiled at 0° and froze at 100°. After his death, it was flipped to the familiar form, possibly by Carl Linnaeus or others."],["Galileo thermometers","The decorative ‘Galileo thermometer’, with colored glass bulbs floating in liquid, was not invented by Galileo but named after him because it uses a principle he studied: the density of liquids changes with temperature."]],
sp:["Hottest and coldest","The highest air temperature accepted by the World Meteorological Organization is 56.7°C, recorded in Death Valley, California, in 1913, though some scientists doubt it. The lowest is −89.2°C at Vostok Station in Antarctica in 1983."],
g:[["Temperature","How hot or cold something is"],["Absolute zero","The lowest possible temperature, −273.15°C"],["Infrared","Heat radiation invisible to the eye"],["Thermocouple","A temperature sensor made of two metals"]],
f:["Celsius and Fahrenheit are equal at −40°.","Celsius’s original scale was upside down.","Mercury thermometers are being phased out."],
fun:"Daniel Fahrenheit set 0° on his scale using a mixture of ice, water and salt, the coldest temperature he could reliably make."});

ADDART({id:"browndwarfs",t:"Brown Dwarfs",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🟤",w:"Brown dwarf",r:["stars","jupiter","exoplanet","jwst","fusion"],
p:[["Failed stars","Brown dwarfs are too massive to be planets but too small to be true stars."],["Not quite shining","They cannot sustain hydrogen fusion, so they slowly cool and fade."],["Discovered in 1995","The first brown dwarfs were confirmed only in 1995."]],
l:"Brown dwarfs are objects in space that are bigger than giant planets but too small to become true stars, often called ‘failed stars’. They form like stars, from collapsing clouds of gas, but have between about 13 and 80 times the mass of Jupiter, not enough to sustain the fusion of ordinary hydrogen in their cores that powers stars like the Sun. Brown dwarfs can briefly fuse a heavy form of hydrogen called deuterium, then gradually cool and fade over billions of years. Despite their name, they appear deep red, magenta or even dark to the human eye, and give off most of their light as infrared. Their existence was predicted by Indian-American astronomer Shiv Kumar in 1962, but they were so faint that the first were confirmed only in 1995: Teide 1 in the Pleiades star cluster and Gliese 229B, a companion to a nearby red dwarf. Thousands are now known. The coldest, such as WISE 0855, are about as cold as the North Pole, and the James Webb Space Telescope has studied their weather of clouds made of hot sand and iron droplets.",
s:[["Between planets and stars","Astronomers usually define a brown dwarf as having between about 13 and 80 Jupiter masses. Below 13, objects cannot fuse even deuterium and are considered planets; above about 80, they become red dwarf stars. Curiously, most brown dwarfs are roughly the same size as Jupiter, because extra mass squeezes them tighter."],["Spectral classes","Astronomers classify brown dwarfs as L, T and Y types as they get cooler. Y dwarfs, the coldest, discovered by NASA’s WISE telescope in 2011, can be colder than 0°C. Methane and water are found in their atmospheres, much like in giant planets."],["Weather","Brown dwarfs rotate quickly and have bands of winds and storms. Observations show they change in brightness as patchy clouds rotate into view. JWST has detected clouds of silicate dust in some of them, which would rain down as molten rock."],["Gliese 229B","Gliese 229B, one of the first brown dwarfs discovered, puzzled astronomers for decades because it seemed too faint for its mass. In 2024, astronomers found that it is actually two brown dwarfs orbiting each other every 12 days."]],
k:[["Mass","About 13–80 Jupiter masses"],["Size","About the size of Jupiter"],["Predicted","Shiv Kumar, 1962"],["First confirmed","1995 (Teide 1, Gliese 229B)"],["Classes","L, T and Y"]],
d:[["1962","Shiv Kumar predicts brown dwarfs"],["1975","The name ‘brown dwarf’ suggested by Jill Tarter"],["1995","First brown dwarfs confirmed"],["2011","WISE discovers Y-type brown dwarfs"],["2014","Coldest known brown dwarf, WISE 0855, found"],["2024","Gliese 229B found to be a pair"]],
s2:[["Why ‘brown’?","Astronomer Jill Tarter suggested the name ‘brown dwarf’ in 1975 for objects between red dwarfs and planets. Brown is not a true color of light, and real brown dwarfs would look dull red or magenta, but the name stuck."],["Free-floating","Many brown dwarfs drift through space alone, not orbiting any star. Some astronomers think the galaxy may contain tens of billions of them, though they are hard to count because they are so faint."]],
sp:["Luhman 16","Luhman 16, discovered in 2013, is a pair of brown dwarfs only about 6.5 light-years away, the closest brown dwarfs to the Sun. Weather maps of one of them show cloud bands like Jupiter’s."],
g:[["Fusion","Joining atomic nuclei, releasing energy"],["Deuterium","A heavy form of hydrogen"],["Infrared","Light with longer wavelengths than red"],["Red dwarf","A small, cool star"]],
f:["Brown dwarfs are called failed stars.","Most brown dwarfs are about the size of Jupiter.","The first brown dwarfs were confirmed in 1995."],
fun:"The coldest brown dwarf known, WISE 0855, is colder than −20°C, cold enough to have water ice clouds."});

ADDART({id:"redgiants",t:"Red Giants",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔴",w:"Red giant",r:["sun","stars","whitedwarfs","betelgeuse","nebula"],
p:[["Swollen old stars","Red giants are stars that have swollen hugely near the end of their lives."],["The Sun’s future","In about 5 billion years, the Sun will become a red giant."],["Making elements","Red giants create carbon and other elements later spread into space."]],
l:"A red giant is a star in a late stage of its life that has expanded to tens or hundreds of times its original size and cooled on the surface, giving it an orange-red color. Stars like the Sun spend most of their lives fusing hydrogen into helium in their cores. When the core runs out of hydrogen, it contracts and heats up, while hydrogen continues fusing in a shell around it, causing the outer layers to swell enormously. Later the core becomes hot enough to fuse helium into carbon and oxygen. Well-known red giants include Arcturus and Aldebaran, among the brightest stars in the night sky; much larger stars become red supergiants, such as Betelgeuse and Antares. In about 5 billion years, the Sun will become a red giant, swelling to perhaps more than 200 times its present size, engulfing Mercury and Venus and possibly Earth. Eventually, red giants shed their outer layers into colorful planetary nebulae, leaving behind white dwarfs.",
s:[["How a star becomes a red giant","As the core shrinks and heats, the energy from shell burning pushes the outer layers outward. Although the star becomes far more luminous, its huge surface is cooler, about 3,000 to 4,500°C, so it glows red-orange. Its outer layers become thin, with a density far less than air."],["The helium flash","In stars like the Sun, helium fusion begins suddenly in a ‘helium flash’, which releases enormous energy deep in the core for a few minutes, though it is not visible from outside. After that, the star settles down to fuse helium more steadily."],["Factories of elements","Red giants produce carbon, nitrogen and heavier elements through slow neutron capture. Their strong winds carry these elements into space, where they become part of new stars and planets. Much of the carbon in living things was made inside red giant stars."],["The Sun’s fate","When the Sun becomes a red giant, its outer layers may reach Earth’s orbit. Whether Earth is swallowed is uncertain, because the Sun will also lose mass, letting the planets move outward. Long before then, in about 1 billion years, the Sun’s growing brightness will boil away Earth’s oceans."]],
k:[["Stage","Late in a star’s life"],["Surface temperature","About 3,000–4,500°C"],["Examples","Arcturus, Aldebaran"],["Supergiant examples","Betelgeuse, Antares"],["Sun becomes one","In about 5 billion years"]],
d:[["1905–1913","Hertzsprung and Russell discover giant and dwarf stars"],["1920","First star size measured, Betelgeuse"],["1950s","Astronomers explain how red giants form"],["1957","Paper explains how stars make elements"],["2023","Scientists observe a star swallowing a planet"]],
s2:[["The Hertzsprung–Russell diagram","Around 1910, Ejnar Hertzsprung and Henry Norris Russell plotted stars’ brightness against their color and found that they fall into groups, including the ‘main sequence’ where stars spend most of their lives and the region of red giants. The diagram became one of the most important tools in astronomy."],["A star eats a planet","In 2023, astronomers reported a burst of light from a star about 12,000 light-years away, which they interpreted as the star swallowing a planet as it expanded into a red giant, a preview of what may happen to some planets in our solar system."]],
sp:["Arcturus","Arcturus, in the constellation Boötes, is a red giant about 25 times the Sun’s diameter and one of the brightest stars visible from Earth. Light from Arcturus was used to switch on the lights of the 1933 Chicago World’s Fair."],
g:[["Red giant","A large, cool, very bright old star"],["Planetary nebula","A glowing shell of gas thrown off by a dying star"],["White dwarf","The hot, dense core left after a red giant"],["Main sequence","The stage when stars fuse hydrogen in their cores"]],
f:["The Sun will become a red giant in about 5 billion years.","Much of the carbon in living things was made in red giants.","Arcturus is a red giant."],
fun:"Betelgeuse is so large that if it replaced the Sun, its surface would extend beyond the orbit of Mars."});

ADDART({id:"phobosdeimos",t:"Phobos and Deimos",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🥔",w:"Moons of Mars",r:["mars","moons","marsrovers","asteroidbelt","impactcraters"],
p:[["The moons of Mars","Mars has two small, potato-shaped moons, Phobos and Deimos."],["Fear and dread","They are named after sons of Ares, the Greek god of war."],["Phobos is doomed","Phobos is slowly spiraling toward Mars and will break apart."]],
l:"Phobos and Deimos are the two small moons of Mars, discovered by American astronomer Asaph Hall in August 1877. They are named after the twin sons of Ares, the Greek god of war: Phobos means ‘fear’ and Deimos ‘dread’. Both are irregular, lumpy bodies, more like potatoes than spheres. Phobos, the larger, is about 27 kilometers across at its widest; Deimos is about 15 kilometers. Phobos orbits only about 6,000 kilometers above the Martian surface, closer than any other moon to its planet, circling Mars three times a day, so that from the surface it rises in the west and sets in the east. Deimos orbits farther out, taking about 30 hours. Their origin is debated: they may be captured asteroids or debris from a giant impact on Mars. Phobos is slowly spiraling inward and will be torn apart by Mars’s gravity in roughly 30 to 50 million years, possibly forming a ring. Japan’s Martian Moons eXploration (MMX) mission, launched in October 2026, aims to land on Phobos and return samples to Earth in 2031.",
s:[["Phobos","Phobos is covered in craters and grooves. Its largest crater, Stickney, is about 9 kilometers wide, almost a third of the moon’s size; the impact that formed it nearly shattered Phobos. Long grooves across its surface may be stretch marks from Mars’s tidal pull or chains of craters from debris."],["Deimos","Deimos is smoother, with fewer visible craters because they are partly buried by loose dust. From Mars, it looks like a bright star and takes about two and a half days to cross the sky because it orbits almost as fast as Mars rotates."],["Origins","The moons’ dark color and composition resemble some asteroids, suggesting capture, but their nearly circular orbits near Mars’s equator are hard to explain that way. Many scientists now favor the idea that they formed from debris thrown into orbit when a large object struck Mars. MMX samples may settle the question."],["Missions","Soviet Phobos 1 and 2 probes in 1988 failed before completing their missions, and Russia’s Phobos-Grunt sample-return mission in 2011 never left Earth orbit. Spacecraft orbiting Mars have photographed both moons in detail, and rovers have watched Phobos pass in front of the Sun."]],
k:[["Discovered","Asaph Hall, August 1877"],["Phobos size","About 27 km across"],["Deimos size","About 15 km across"],["Phobos orbit","About 6,000 km above Mars, three times a day"],["Sample mission","MMX (launched 2026)"]],
d:[["1726","Gulliver’s Travels imagines two moons of Mars"],["1877","Asaph Hall discovers Phobos and Deimos"],["1971","Mariner 9 photographs the moons up close"],["1988","Soviet Phobos probes fail"],["2011","Phobos-Grunt fails in Earth orbit"],["2026","Japan launches MMX"]],
s2:[["Swift’s prediction","In Gulliver’s Travels (1726), Jonathan Swift described astronomers of Laputa who had discovered two moons of Mars, more than 150 years before they were found. It was a lucky guess, possibly based on an idea of Johannes Kepler."],["Eclipses on Mars","NASA’s rovers have filmed Phobos crossing the Sun, creating a partial eclipse shaped like a potato. Because Phobos is too small to cover the Sun completely, Mars never has total solar eclipses."]],
sp:["Asaph Hall’s discovery","Asaph Hall searched for Martian moons with the US Naval Observatory’s new telescope in 1877. He almost gave up, but his wife, Angeline Stickney, encouraged him to continue. He found Deimos on 12 August and Phobos on 18 August. Phobos’s largest crater is named Stickney in her honor."],
g:[["Captured asteroid","An asteroid pulled into orbit by a planet’s gravity"],["Tidal force","The stretching effect of gravity"],["Groove","A long, narrow channel"],["Sample return","Bringing material from space to Earth"]],
f:["Phobos orbits Mars three times a day.","Phobos will break apart in tens of millions of years.","Mars never has total solar eclipses."],
fun:"From the surface of Mars, Phobos rises in the west and sets in the east, crossing the sky in about four and a half hours."});

ADDART({id:"gammaraybursts",t:"Gamma-Ray Bursts",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"💥",w:"Gamma-ray burst",r:["supernova","bh","pulsar","gravwaves","radioastronomy"],
p:[["The brightest explosions","Gamma-ray bursts are the most powerful explosions known in the universe."],["Found by spy satellites","Satellites watching for nuclear tests discovered them in 1967."],["Dying stars and colliding stars","They come from collapsing giant stars and merging neutron stars."]],
l:"Gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) are extremely powerful flashes of gamma rays, the most energetic form of light, coming from distant galaxies. In a few seconds, a gamma-ray burst can release more energy than the Sun will produce in its entire 10-billion-year life. They were discovered by accident in 1967 by American Vela satellites designed to detect secret nuclear weapons tests, and the discovery was announced in 1973. For decades their source was a mystery, until in 1997 the Italian–Dutch BeppoSAX satellite detected an afterglow that allowed astronomers to show they come from billions of light-years away. Long gamma-ray bursts, lasting more than about two seconds, occur when massive stars collapse to form black holes, launching narrow jets of matter at nearly the speed of light. Short bursts come from the merger of two neutron stars, as confirmed in 2017 when one was detected together with gravitational waves. On 9 October 2022, astronomers observed GRB 221009A, nicknamed the BOAT, ‘brightest of all time’.",
s:[["Long and short bursts","Long bursts are linked to supernovae of very massive, rapidly spinning stars, in an event called a collapsar. Short bursts, lasting less than two seconds, come from colliding neutron stars or a neutron star and a black hole. In both cases, jets aimed toward Earth make the bursts look especially bright."],["Afterglows","After the gamma rays fade, the jets crash into surrounding gas, producing afterglows in X-rays, visible light and radio waves that can last for days or weeks. NASA’s Swift satellite, launched in 2004, quickly points its telescopes at new bursts to catch afterglows."],["GW170817","On 17 August 2017, gravitational-wave detectors recorded two neutron stars merging about 130 million light-years away, and less than two seconds later a short gamma-ray burst was detected. Telescopes worldwide then observed a ‘kilonova’ that created heavy elements such as gold and platinum."],["The BOAT","GRB 221009A, seen in October 2022, was so bright that it disturbed Earth’s upper atmosphere and blinded many gamma-ray detectors. Astronomers estimate a burst that bright happens only about once every 10,000 years."]],
k:[["Light type","Gamma rays"],["Discovered","1967 by Vela satellites (announced 1973)"],["Long bursts","Collapsing massive stars"],["Short bursts","Merging neutron stars"],["Brightest seen","GRB 221009A, 2022"]],
d:[["1967","Vela satellites detect the first gamma-ray burst"],["1973","Discovery announced"],["1997","BeppoSAX finds an afterglow, proving bursts are distant"],["2004","Swift satellite launched"],["2017","Short burst seen with gravitational waves"],["2022","The BOAT, the brightest burst ever seen"]],
s2:[["A danger to life?","If a gamma-ray burst occurred within a few thousand light-years and pointed at Earth, its radiation could destroy much of the ozone layer, exposing life to harmful ultraviolet light. Some scientists have suggested a burst might have contributed to a mass extinction about 440 million years ago, though there is no direct evidence."],["Distant beacons","Because they are so bright, gamma-ray bursts can be seen across almost the whole observable universe. GRB 090423, detected in 2009, exploded when the universe was only about 630 million years old."]],
sp:["Spy satellites","The Vela satellites were launched by the United States after the 1963 treaty banning nuclear tests in the atmosphere and space. Their detectors recorded unexpected gamma-ray flashes that did not match nuclear explosions; scientists kept the discovery secret until they were sure the flashes came from space."],
g:[["Gamma ray","The most energetic form of light"],["Afterglow","Fading light after a burst"],["Kilonova","An explosion from merging neutron stars"],["Jet","A narrow, fast stream of matter"]],
f:["Gamma-ray bursts are the most powerful explosions known.","They were found by satellites watching for nuclear tests.","The brightest burst ever seen was in 2022."],
fun:"A single gamma-ray burst can briefly outshine all the other stars in the universe put together, as seen in gamma rays."});

ADDART({id:"cosmicrays",t:"Cosmic Rays",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"⚛️",w:"Cosmic ray",r:["radioact","sun","atom","carbondating","supernova"],
p:[["Particles from space","Cosmic rays are fast-moving particles that rain on Earth from space."],["Discovered by balloon","Victor Hess discovered them on a balloon flight in 1912."],["Mysterious origins","The most energetic cosmic rays come from unknown sources."]],
l:"Cosmic rays are high-energy particles, mostly protons and the nuclei of atoms, that travel through space at nearly the speed of light and constantly strike Earth’s atmosphere. Despite the name, they are not rays of light. Austrian physicist Victor Hess discovered them in 1912, when he carried radiation detectors on balloon flights and found that radiation increased with altitude, showing that it came from space; he shared the 1936 Nobel Prize in Physics. Cosmic rays come from the Sun, from supernovae and other violent events in our galaxy, and the most energetic come from beyond the Milky Way. When they hit atoms in the upper atmosphere, they create showers of secondary particles, such as muons, millions of which pass through each of us every day. Cosmic rays produce the carbon-14 used in radiocarbon dating, can flip bits in computer memory, and are a radiation risk for astronauts and airline crews. The ‘Oh-My-God particle’ detected in 1991 and the ‘Amaterasu particle’ announced in 2023 had energies millions of times greater than any particle accelerator can produce.",
s:[["Air showers","A single cosmic ray hitting the atmosphere can create a cascade of billions of particles spreading over many square kilometers. Large observatories, such as the Pierre Auger Observatory in Argentina, completed in 2008, use detectors spread over 3,000 square kilometers to measure these showers."],["Where they come from","Lower-energy cosmic rays come from the Sun, especially during solar flares. Most others are thought to be accelerated by shock waves from supernova explosions. The origins of the highest-energy ‘ultra-high-energy’ cosmic rays, which may come from distant active galaxies, remain uncertain because magnetic fields bend their paths."],["Effects on Earth","Earth’s magnetic field and atmosphere shield us from most cosmic radiation. Air crews on long high-altitude flights receive higher doses than people on the ground. Cosmic rays occasionally cause errors in computer chips, which matters for aircraft and spacecraft electronics."],["Muon imaging","Muons from cosmic rays can pass through rock. Scientists use them like X-rays to image the insides of volcanoes and pyramids. In 2017, muon imaging revealed a large hidden void inside the Great Pyramid of Giza."]],
k:[["Made of","Mostly protons and atomic nuclei"],["Discovered","Victor Hess, 1912"],["Nobel Prize","1936"],["Most famous particle","Oh-My-God particle, 1991"],["Uses","Carbon-14 dating, muon imaging"]],
d:[["1912","Victor Hess discovers cosmic rays by balloon"],["1936","Hess shares the Nobel Prize in Physics"],["1991","Oh-My-God particle detected in Utah"],["2008","Pierre Auger Observatory completed"],["2017","Muons reveal a void in the Great Pyramid"],["2023","Amaterasu particle announced"]],
s2:[["The Oh-My-God particle","On 15 October 1991, the Fly’s Eye detector in Utah recorded a cosmic ray with an energy of about 3 × 10²⁰ electron volts, equal to a baseball thrown at about 90 kilometers per hour, packed into a single subatomic particle. Scientists still do not know what produced it."],["Cosmic rays and astronauts","Beyond Earth’s magnetic field, astronauts are exposed to far more cosmic radiation, which can damage DNA and increase cancer risk. Protecting crews on long missions to Mars is one of the biggest challenges of space travel."]],
sp:["Seeing cosmic rays","Apollo astronauts reported seeing flashes of light with their eyes closed. Scientists concluded that these were caused by cosmic rays passing through their eyes and striking the retina."],
g:[["Cosmic ray","A high-energy particle from space"],["Muon","A heavier cousin of the electron created in air showers"],["Air shower","A cascade of particles from a cosmic ray impact"],["Electron volt","A unit of energy used for particles"]],
f:["Cosmic rays are particles, not rays of light.","Millions of muons pass through you every day.","Cosmic rays make carbon-14."],
fun:"About one muon created by cosmic rays passes through every square centimeter of Earth’s surface each minute."});

ADDART({id:"ionengines",t:"Ion Engines",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔵",w:"Ion thruster",r:["rocket","ceres","asteroidbelt","megaconstellations","solarsails"],
p:[["Electric rockets","Ion engines push spacecraft using electrically charged atoms."],["Gentle but tireless","Their thrust is tiny, but they can run for years."],["Deep-space travel","Ion engines carried spacecraft such as Dawn to the asteroid belt."]],
l:"An ion engine, or ion thruster, is a type of electric propulsion that moves spacecraft by accelerating charged atoms, called ions, to very high speeds using electricity. Most ion engines use xenon gas: electrons are knocked off the xenon atoms, giving them a positive charge, and charged grids or magnetic fields shoot them out of the engine at up to about 40 kilometers per second, more than ten times faster than exhaust from chemical rockets. The push is extremely gentle, about the weight of a sheet of paper on a hand, so ion engines cannot lift rockets off the ground. But in space, they can fire for months or years using little fuel, gradually reaching very high speeds. NASA’s Deep Space 1 tested an ion engine on an interplanetary mission in 1998, and the Dawn spacecraft used ion propulsion to orbit two worlds, Vesta and Ceres. Japan’s Hayabusa probes, Europe’s BepiColombo to Mercury and NASA’s Psyche, heading to a metal-rich asteroid, all use electric propulsion, as do thousands of satellites, including many Starlink satellites.",
s:[["How it works","Electricity, usually from solar panels, powers the engine. In a gridded ion engine, xenon is ionized and the ions are pulled through electrically charged grids, which accelerate them. A Hall-effect thruster uses a magnetic field to trap electrons that ionize and accelerate the gas. Electrons are added back to the exhaust so the spacecraft does not build up an electric charge."],["Efficiency","Rocket engineers measure fuel efficiency by ‘specific impulse’. Chemical rockets reach about 450 seconds at best, while ion engines achieve 3,000 seconds or more, meaning they get far more change in speed from each kilogram of propellant."],["Dawn","Dawn, launched in 2007, orbited the giant asteroid Vesta in 2011–2012 and then flew on to the dwarf planet Ceres, which it orbited from 2015 until 2018. It was the first spacecraft to orbit two different bodies beyond Earth, something possible only with its ion engines, which fired for a total of almost six years."],["The future","Ion engines are increasingly used to move satellites into their final orbits and keep them in place. Engineers are developing more powerful electric thrusters, and some cheaper ones use krypton or iodine instead of rare xenon. Nuclear-electric propulsion could one day power crewed missions."]],
k:[["Propellant","Usually xenon"],["Exhaust speed","Up to about 40 km/s"],["Thrust","Very gentle"],["First interplanetary test","Deep Space 1, 1998"],["Famous user","Dawn (Vesta and Ceres)"]],
d:[["1911","Konstantin Tsiolkovsky writes about electric propulsion"],["1959","Harold Kaufman builds an early ion engine at NASA"],["1998","Deep Space 1 launches with an ion engine"],["2003","Japan’s Hayabusa launches with ion engines"],["2007","Dawn launches"],["2023","Psyche launches to a metal-rich asteroid"]],
s2:[["Science fiction","Ion engines appeared in science fiction long before they flew. In Star Wars, the TIE fighters’ name stands for ‘Twin Ion Engines’, and the glowing blue exhaust of real ion engines looks remarkably like the films."],["Psyche","NASA’s Psyche spacecraft, launched in October 2023, uses Hall-effect thrusters to travel to the asteroid 16 Psyche, which may be the exposed metal core of an early planet. It is due to arrive in 2029."]],
sp:["The blue glow","Ion engines produce a ghostly blue glow from excited xenon ions. Photos of the engines being tested in vacuum chambers show beams of soft blue light."],
g:[["Ion","An atom with an electric charge"],["Xenon","A heavy, unreactive gas"],["Specific impulse","A measure of rocket fuel efficiency"],["Hall-effect thruster","An ion engine using magnetic fields"]],
f:["Ion engines push with about the force of a sheet of paper.","Dawn orbited both Vesta and Ceres.","Ion engines usually use xenon gas."],
fun:"Over its mission, Dawn’s ion engines increased its speed by about 11.5 kilometers per second, more than any other spacecraft’s own propulsion at the time."});
